Fix the dictionary key boost on shifted keyboards and on accented words.

Two independent defects in FUTO Keyboard 0.1.30's "smart key-hit detection":

1. KeyDetector compared a lowercase dictionary code point set against key.getCode(),
   but a shifted keyboard rebuilds every shiftable key with an uppercased code
   (BaseKey#computeDataWithExtraAttrs), so the boost was silently disabled for the whole
   word while Shift or Caps Lock was active. Fixed by lowercasing the key code before the
   membership test; the extra lookup can only match letters that are already in the set.

2. processGetValidNextCodePoints compared code points exactly, while the rest of the engine
   compares base lowercase code points so that accent errors are tolerated. Typing an
   unaccented spelling of an accented word therefore dropped the boost for the rest of the
   word, and an accented child ("é") armed a code point that no base key has. Fixed with an
   exact-first walk that only falls back to the base letter when no exact child exists, and by
   emitting both the accented and the base form of every child.

Measured with the offline harness in harness/ (Gaussian taps over the real Spanish layout,
sigma 10 dp): plain words unchanged at 2.36 % error, caps-lock words from 5.29 % to 2.54 %,
accented words typed unaccented from 4.54 % to 4.45 %, out-of-dictionary unchanged. No
probability weighting is included: the harness shows weighted margins are worse without a
calibrated weight source.

Generated against FUTO Keyboard tag 0.1.30 (0246cbe). Applies after futo-keyboard-polyglot
patches 0001-0007 with: git apply --3way --whitespace=nowarn

Produced with AI assistance. The main agent was pi (gentle-shell) running deepseek-v4.1-flash,
with review from two pi-fabric advisors (Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6.1-sol). FUTO's README does not
accept AI generated pull requests, so read the fork's README before sending anything upstream.

